<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION" />

  <application>
    <service
      android:name=".ShiftTimerService"
      android:exported="false"
      android:stopWithTask="false"
      android:foregroundServiceType="location" />

    <!--
      No foregroundServiceType, deliberately, and it is not an omission.

      This is a HeadlessJsTaskService: it never calls startForeground, so it
      is not a foreground service and Android 14's typed-service requirement
      does not apply to it. It is started with startService (see
      ShiftTimerService.kt line ~147), not startForegroundService — and that
      call is made from inside ShiftTimerService, which IS a foreground
      service, so Android 12's background-start restriction is satisfied by
      exemption rather than by luck.

      Declaring a type here would be WORSE than leaving it off: it would
      assert a foreground service that never starts, and Android 14 kills a
      typed service that does not call startForeground in time.

      The long-lived ShiftTimerService above is the real foreground service
      and is correctly typed "location".
    -->
    <service
      android:name=".ShiftTimerTickService"
      android:exported="false" />

    <receiver
      android:name=".ShiftTimerAlarmReceiver"
      android:exported="false" />
  </application>
</manifest>
